\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{The Rubik group and labelled configurations}

\p{For a finite \vocabk{regular abstract polytope}{frap-000G} #{P} of rank #{n\geq3},
the \newvocab{Rubik group} is the subgroup generated by all
\vocabk{facet moves}{frap-000L}:}
##{\operatorname{Rub}(P)=
\langle\mu_{H,\alpha}:H\text{ a facet},\quad
\alpha\in\Gamma^+(H/F_{-1})\rangle
\leq\operatorname{Sym}(\mathcal R(P)).}
\p{This is Marchetti's generated \vocabk{group}{ca-000Q} of permutations
\citet{Section 3, p. 6, following Definition 6}{marchetti2025rubik}.
The [inverse lemma](frap-000M) ensures that the generators are permutations.}

\p{Give each incidence position its own initial label. A
\newvocab{labelled configuration} can be represented by the \vocabk{permutation}{frap-000L}
carrying each initial label to its current position. The identity is the
solved configuration; the reachable labelled configurations are the
elements of #{\operatorname{Rub}(P)}. A new move acts on the left:
applying #{\mu} to configuration #{g} gives #{\mu g}, with #{g} acting
first. A visible coloring may identify several labelled configurations,
so a solved visible coloring need not determine the labelled permutation.}
